<spell-notebook "radio usage">
	<header level="1">
		Radio
		<header>A radio button lets a user choose exactly one value of a group.</>
	</>
	<radio-tab-bar showing="usage"/>
	<section>
		<menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
			<item link="#using-it">Using it</>
			<item link="#radio-groups">Radio groups</>
			<item link="#forms">Forms</>
			<item link="#events">Events</>
			<item link="#keyboard">Keyboard</>
			<item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
			<item link="#framework-usage">Framework usage</>
		</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  groups, forms, `selected` / `checked`, events, keyboard, accessibility, frameworks. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			Write one `<ui-radio>` per option, its label inside, and give the options of one question the same `name`. No
			JavaScript to call: radios work as soon as the elements are defined, like Fomantic's radio checkbox without its
			module. `<ui-radio>` shares its family, sheet and tokens with [`<ui-checkbox>`](/components/ui/ui-checkbox.html).

			- `appearance="toggle"` / `appearance="slider"` changes the look; the default is the round radio.
			- `selected` is the chosen state, as an attribute and a property. `checked` is an alias: the `checked`
			  attribute in markup chooses the radio, and `el.checked` reads and writes `el.selected`.
			- The label is the slotted content, or `label` for plain text; a `padding="none"` radio has none.
			- Wrap a group in `<ui-fields grouped>` (one per line) or `<ui-fields inline>` (one row) inside a `<ui-form>`.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Radio groups">
		<markdown>
			Radios group by `name` within their form, or the whole page when there is none, even though each `<ui-radio>`
			keeps its input in its own shadow root. Choosing one unchooses the others without firing events on them. A group
			can mix looks (a radio, a slider and a toggle with one `name` are still one group).
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Forms">
		<markdown>
			Radios are real form controls.

			- A group submits `name=value` of its chosen radio (`value` defaults to `on`, as natively); with none chosen,
			  nothing.
			- `form.reset()` restores the starting choice, and a disabled `<fieldset>` disables them.
			- `required` on any member makes the whole group required.
			- `disabled` leaves a radio out of the form; `readonly` keeps it in but cancels clicks.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Events">
		<markdown>
			`ui-changed` fires on the newly chosen radio only, when the user chooses it, with `{ selected, value,
			originalEvent }`. It fires BEFORE the change, so a handler that sets `selected` back vetoes it (see the
			checkbox page's [Vetoing a change](/components/ui/ui-checkbox.html)). Setting `selected` from code
			fires nothing -- Fomantic's `set checked`.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			const form = document.querySelector("form")
			form.addEventListener("ui-changed", (event) => {
			  if (event.target.localName === "ui-radio") console.log(event.target.name, event.detail.value)
			})
			document.querySelector("ui-radio[value=day]").checked = true // same as .selected = true;  no event
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Keyboard">
		<markdown>
			- **Space** chooses the focused radio.
			- **Arrow Down** / **Arrow Right**: choose the next enabled radio of the group and focus it (wraps).
			- **Arrow Up** / **Arrow Left**: choose the previous enabled radio of the group (wraps).
			- **Tab** moves into or out of a group, through its one tab stop.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			- The shadow root holds a real `<input type="radio">` tied to a `<label>`, so clicking the label, Space, and the
			  checked state come from the browser. `delegatesFocus` keeps the host out of the tab order and draws the focus
			  ring on the box.
			- Toggles and sliders keep the radio role (one of a group), not `switch`.
			- The accessible name is the label text. A `padding="none"` radio (or an `invisible` one holding only an image) has none,
			  so give it an `aria-label` or a `<label for>` pointing at the host.
			- Wrap a group in `role="radiogroup"` with a name (`aria-label` or `aria-labelledby`), as the examples do.
			- Only the chosen radio of a group (else the first enabled one) is a tab stop, as with native radios.
			- A required group the user left empty gets `aria-invalid="true"` once they have interacted.
			- If the element fails to render, its native fallback keeps the same markup with a plain native radio:
			  it still submits, validates `required` and fires `ui-changed`. It drops grouping across elements
			  (each fallback radio stands alone, with no arrow keys), vetoing `ui-changed`, the invalid state and
			  reset to the starting state.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Framework usage">
		<markdown>Bind `selected` as a property on each radio, and listen for `ui-changed` on the group.</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-react">React 19</>
		<markdown>
			```jsx
			{sizes.map((size) => (
			  <ui-radio key={size} name="size" value={size} selected={chosen === size} onui-changed={() => setChosen(size)}>
			    {size}
			  </ui-radio>
			))}
			```
		</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-vue">Vue 3.5</>
		<markdown>
			```html
			<!-- compilerOptions.isCustomElement = (tag) => tag.startsWith("ui-") -->
			<ui-radio v-for="size in sizes" name="size" :value="size" :selected="chosen === size" @ui-changed="chosen = size">
			  {{ size }}
			</ui-radio>
			```
		</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-solid">Solid 2</>
		<markdown>
			```jsx
			// events and rich data are plain props:  `on-<event>={...}`, `options={...}`
			<For each={sizes}>
			  {(size) => (
			    <ui-radio
			      name="size"
			      value={size()}
			      selected={chosen() === size()}
			      on-ui-changed={() => setChosen(size())}
			    >
			      {size()}
			    </ui-radio>
			  )}
			</For>
			```
		</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
